<<<
+ lime for some-one to raise a genile protest. ‘ Very much affronted’, or * highly affronted’, if you like, but surely not ‘very affronted’. Try another tense: one cannot say * your language very, affronts me’;
===
+ *time for some-one to raise a gentle protest. 'Very much affronted', or 'highly affronted', if you like, but surely not 'very affronted'. Try another tense: one cannot say 'your language very affronts me';
>>>

<<<
but I can say (with truth) ‘ your language very much affronts me’. How reasonable it sounds: see different for a similar argument; but it proves too much; similarly I cannot say This very tires me; & yet, dissimilarly, I can say I am very tired;
===
but I can say (with truth) 'your language very much affronts me'.* How reasonable it sounds: see *different* for a similar argument; but it proves too much; similarly I cannot say *This very tires me*; & yet, dissimilarly, I can say *I am very tired*;
>>>

<<<
& with affronted itself I can say He wore a very affronted look; & His look was very affronted is, if not a likely expression, at least better than His look was much affronted.
===
& with *affronted* itself I can say *He wore a very affronted look*; & *His look was very affronted* is, if not a likely expression, at least better than *His look was much affronted*.
>>>

